          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Repeated urine infections are worth discussing with a clinician, who can look at your history and decide what testing, if any, makes sense. I can't tell you whether you have a particular condition.\n\n**What the guidance says:**\n- The general clinical reference (Urinary tract infection) lists factors that can contribute to urine infections. These include a shorter urethra, sexual activity, certain contraceptives such as spermicides and diaphragms, changes in vaginal flora after menopause, not fully emptying the bladder, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections.\n- The infectious diseases and urology guidance (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence) says a urine culture is used when it would change treatment. One of those cases is recurrent infection. That guidance also says treatment choice depends on local resistance patterns.\n\n**What these passages don't cover:** They don't explain how to prevent repeat infections or which options suit a particular person. They also don't say which tests or treatments fit your situation. A clinician is the right person to ask about those.\n\n**Seek urgent care right away** if you have a urine infection and any of these: confusion or unusual drowsiness, fast or hard breathing, a fast heartbeat or dizziness on standing, not passing urine for many hours, skin that looks mottled, pale or blue, shivering or a very high or low temperature, or a feeling of being far more unwell than ever before. These can be signs of sepsis, a serious response to infection (source: Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection, critical care and emergency medicine society guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
