          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there anything that reduces how often they happen?",
          "answer": "The passages I found don't say what reduces how often urine infections happen.\n\nThe general clinical reference (Urinary tract infection) says that recurrence prevention is covered by a separate urinary tract infection guideline note. That note did not come up in my search, so I can't report what it says. The reference also lists factors that can contribute to infections, such as not fully emptying the bladder, kidney stones, diabetes, and certain contraceptives. Those are background factors, not prevention advice, and I can't tell you which, if any, apply to you.\n\nA clinician who knows your history is the best person to ask. Useful questions include: \"What could be causing my infections to come back?\" and \"Would any tests help find the cause?\"\n\nSeek urgent care right away if a urine infection comes with confusion, fast or hard breathing, a fast heartbeat or dizziness on standing, not passing urine for many hours, mottled, pale or blue skin, shivering, or a feeling of being much more unwell than usual.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
