          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "I can't tell you whether you need an antibiotic for your own infections. The passages I found don't say when antibiotics are or aren't needed for a given person.\n\nHere is what the retrieved passages do say:\n\n- **Treatment is guided by local resistance patterns.** The general clinical reference (Urinary tract infection) says antibiotic choice depends on local resistance.\n- **Experts differ on immediate treatment.** The infectious diseases and urology guidance (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence) says some European guidance offers symptom relief first, with antibiotics delayed or not given, as a shared decision. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default. The guidance says the disagreement is about how much antibiotic use is worth avoiding.\n- **Symptom relief supports treatment.** The general clinical reference says fluids and symptomatic relief support treatment but don't replace it.\n\nAsk a clinician whether your next infection needs an antibiotic, and whether a urine test would help decide. Seek prompt assessment if you have fever with back or side pain, shaking chills, vomiting that stops you drinking, confusion, or symptoms that get worse after 48 hours of treatment. Seek emergency care straight away for confusion, fast or hard breathing, a fast heartbeat or dizziness on standing, not passing urine for many hours, mottled, pale or blue skin, or a feeling of being far more unwell than usual.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
